<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pokhran</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pokhran is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near which lies the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>'s test range. India carried out its first nuclear test there on 18 May 1974, code-named Smiling Buddha, and five further tests in May 1998, after which it declared itself a nuclear-weapon state; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> answered with its own tests weeks later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pokhran.jpg" alt="Pokhran" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archan dave · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pokhran_Fort.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pokhran, officially spelled Pokaran, is a town and municipality in the Jaisalmer district of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. It lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a>, 112 km east of the city of Jaisalmer, 172 km north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> and 225 km south of Bikaner by road, on the railway line from Jaisalmer to Jodhpur. In Rajasthani the name means a place surrounded by five salt ranges. The climate is arid, with little rain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> months of July to September, extremely hot and dry summers in which daytime temperatures can reach 46°C, and very cold winters. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave the municipal board territory a population of 28,457.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pokhran was the seat of the chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champawat">Champawats</a>, a sub-clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathores</a> of the state of Marwar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>. The last jagirdar of Pokhran before Indian independence was Bhawani Singh of Pokhran, born in 1911. He served as a sessions judge and was elected as an independent to the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a> from the Barmer-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a> constituency in the general election of 1952.</p><p>The Pokhran Test Range, a key part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s nuclear programme, lies outside the municipal board's jurisdiction and is controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>. Its army base, built by the Army Corps of Engineers, stands 26 km north-west of the town and 4 km north of the village of Khetolai. It was built before May 1974, when, on the authorisation of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> to the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre, it hosted the detonation of India's first nuclear device.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Ministry of External Affairs designated the test of 18 May 1974 Pokhran-I, and it is also known as Smiling Buddha. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> detonated the bomb on the range under the supervision of several key generals. On 11 and 13 May 1998 the Defence Research and Development Organisation and the Atomic Energy Commission carried out five further tests there, called Pokhran-II: four devices of the Atomic Energy Commission and, under the codename Shakti, a thermonuclear device. The town and the test range are now known chiefly for these tests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pokhran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>India tested a nuclear device at <b>Pokhran</b> in 1974 and declared itself a nuclear-weapon state after further tests in May 1998; Pakistan replied with its own tests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Military</a></li></ul></section>
